WEBVTT

00:00.320 --> 00:02.879
We present DexOAK that retargets

00:02.879 --> 00:04.920
human motion capture into object

00:04.920 --> 00:07.359
aware kinematic references, enabling

00:07.359 --> 00:09.760
replay on real hardware, like this

00:09.760 --> 00:11.400
sequence of an Allegro hand

00:11.400 --> 00:13.400
reproducing a human demonstration

00:13.400 --> 00:14.760
to remove a plug.

00:16.972 --> 00:19.572
Standard kinematic retargeting copies the

00:19.572 --> 00:20.692
human hand pose,

00:21.012 --> 00:23.372
but human robot embodiment differences

00:23.372 --> 00:25.812
can produce the wrong grasp missing the

00:25.812 --> 00:26.412
object.

00:26.892 --> 00:29.452
Thus, we suggest to retarget the hand

00:29.452 --> 00:31.572
object interaction directly at the

00:31.572 --> 00:33.092
kinematic retargeting stage.

00:34.196 --> 00:37.196
Hence, we propose DexOAK. It

00:37.196 --> 00:39.235
takes human hand key points and

00:39.235 --> 00:41.396
an object point cloud as input,

00:41.396 --> 00:43.796
retargets the interaction geometry,

00:43.796 --> 00:45.475
then learns and refines this

00:45.475 --> 00:47.796
mapping with contact losses. The

00:47.796 --> 00:49.675
resulting kinematic trajectory is

00:49.675 --> 00:51.436
made dynamically feasible with

00:51.436 --> 00:52.956
residual RL.

00:53.526 --> 00:56.046
DexOAK handles single-hand and

00:56.046 --> 00:58.886
bimanual tasks, rigid and articulated

00:58.886 --> 01:01.366
objects and different embodiments

01:01.366 --> 01:03.006
from the 16 degree of freedom

01:03.006 --> 01:04.805
Allegro to the 6 degree of

01:04.805 --> 01:06.686
freedom Inspire hands.

01:06.967 --> 01:10.047
Our learned retargeter also generalizes

01:10.047 --> 01:10.927
zero shot.

01:11.287 --> 01:14.487
Trained only on OakInk-v2, it performs

01:14.487 --> 01:17.327
close to the full ARCTIC-trained pipeline

01:17.487 --> 01:20.047
while running nearly 80 times faster.

01:20.568 --> 01:22.808
ManipTrans retargets the hand

01:22.808 --> 01:24.768
alone, so the reference misses the

01:24.768 --> 01:27.408
cap and RL fails early.

01:27.408 --> 01:29.088
DexOAK places the fingers

01:29.088 --> 01:31.248
correctly from the start, enabling

01:31.248 --> 01:33.128
RL to complete the motion.

01:33.129 --> 01:35.809
We see the same trend on ARCTIC with the

01:35.809 --> 01:36.809
Allegro hand.

01:37.169 --> 01:40.169
DexMachina uses hand-only retargeting and

01:40.169 --> 01:42.329
achieves less natural finger placement

01:42.329 --> 01:44.169
along with poorer object motion.

01:44.689 --> 01:47.409
DexOAK is object aware from the start.

01:47.610 --> 01:49.930
We replay successful simulation

01:49.930 --> 01:52.570
roll-outs open loop on a UR5 plus

01:52.570 --> 01:54.690
right Allegro hand with the object

01:54.690 --> 01:56.090
manually initialized at the

01:56.090 --> 01:58.209
demonstration start pose. This

01:58.209 --> 02:00.289
tests feasibility, not policy

02:00.289 --> 02:01.690
generalization.

02:01.690 --> 02:04.050
Across cutting, pouring, placing

02:04.050 --> 02:05.969
and closing tasks, the robot

02:05.969 --> 02:07.889
reproduces the demonstrated contacts

02:07.889 --> 02:10.450
in 46 of 56 trials.

02:10.489 --> 02:12.729
We also test two interactions

02:12.729 --> 02:15.089
unseen during retargeter training,

02:15.089 --> 02:16.848
closing a laptop and pressing a

02:16.848 --> 02:19.849
button. DexOAK retargets both zero

02:19.849 --> 02:21.929
shot before the usual RL

02:21.929 --> 02:24.048
refinement and robot replay,

02:24.048 --> 02:25.288
showing that the kinematic

02:25.288 --> 02:27.409
retargeter generalizes beyond its

02:27.409 --> 02:29.489
training interactions, successfully

02:29.489 --> 02:32.089
replaying 20 out of 24 trials on

02:32.089 --> 02:33.089
real hardware.

02:35.512 --> 02:37.792
Some failures remain. In

02:37.792 --> 02:40.071
simulation, fast motions such as

02:40.071 --> 02:42.312
shaking expose the gap between

02:42.312 --> 02:44.512
kinematics and dynamics. The

02:44.512 --> 02:46.472
reference captures the motion, but

02:46.472 --> 02:48.432
not the required force.

02:48.432 --> 02:50.232
Similar issues appear on the real

02:50.232 --> 02:52.672
robot during cutting or unplugging

02:52.672 --> 02:54.352
where abrupt motions can cause

02:54.352 --> 02:56.471
slip, amplified by open loop

02:56.471 --> 02:58.000
sensitivity to initialization.
